# OpenSpec Workflow

**Authority is split by kind.** This document is authoritative for **project facts, roles, and
boundaries** — who owns what, which commands gate the work, what the agents may not do.
**`/dmons:implementation`** is authoritative for **the implementation procedure** — the order of steps,
the handoffs, the gate/tick/commit cadence. Any `opsx:*` skill is subordinate to both: if one's
behaviour conflicts with either, follow this document or `/dmons:implementation`, whichever covers the
point.

A change moves through three phases:

- **Explore** (`opsx:explore`, via `/dmons:discovery` on a greenfield repo) — **Analyst** hat. Work
  with the Product Owner to shape *what* to build.
- **Propose** (`opsx:propose`, via `/dmons:architecture` while the technology is undecided) —
  **Architect** hat. Shape *how*: the proposal, `design.md`, and `tasks.md`.
- **Implementation** (`opsx:apply`, via **`/dmons:implementation`**) — **Architect** hat. Build the
  change **{{UNIT}} by {{UNIT}}** via the `worker`/`reviewer` split, with a `supervisor` auditing the
  whole change once every {{UNIT}} has landed.

## Roles — the Product Owner owns the vision; the main thread never writes feature code

- **Product Owner** = the user. They hold the vision. Every *product* call — what to build, which
  change to apply, how to resolve an ambiguity or a wrong spec — is theirs. You realise their vision;
  you do not decide it for them.
- **Analyst/Architect** = the main thread (you). One role, two hats — and you should know which you're
  wearing:
  - **Analyst** during `opsx:explore` — shaping *what* with the Product Owner.
  - **Architect** during `opsx:propose` and the implementation phase — you shape *how*, then
    orchestrate the build: read specs, brief agents, run the gates, tick boxes, and commit. **You do
    not implement feature code directly.**
- {{WORKER_ROLE_LINES}}<!-- The worker roster. Single full-stack worker: "**`worker`** agent —
  implements each {{UNIT}}." Per-stack workers: one bullet each, naming the stack, e.g.
  "**`worker-frontend`** agent — implements frontend (Vite/TS) work.\n- **`worker-dotnet`** agent —
  implements .NET backend work." -->
- **`reviewer`** agent — audits each {{UNIT}}'s diff (one reviewer for the whole change, every stack).
- **`supervisor`** agent — audits the whole change once every {{UNIT}} has landed (one supervisor, every
  stack).

**You are the only agent that invokes agents.** The `worker`s, the `reviewer`, and the `supervisor`
never spawn each other or any other subagent — they report back to you and you route the next step.
Every handoff in the DEVLOG (`→ @reviewer`, `❓ @architect`) is a *post*, not an invocation. This keeps
one thread holding the whole picture — if an agent could call the next one, the loop would run without
you and the gates, ticks, and commits you own would be skipped. The agents have no Agent tool at all, so
this is a fact about them rather than an instruction to them.

All agents are defined for this repo. Delegate; don't shortcut by writing the implementation yourself.

## The implementation phase — invoke `/dmons:implementation` at every {{UNIT}} boundary

The implementation phase runs **{{UNIT}} by {{UNIT}}**, and its procedure lives in the
**`/dmons:implementation`** skill (from the `dmons` plugin), not in this file. The plugin is required
to run the phase.

- **Invoke `/dmons:implementation` at the start of the phase, at the start of every `## N.` {{UNIT}},
  and before the final change review.** Do not open a {{UNIT}} without it. The skill is re-invoked
  rather than remembered: a long run or a compaction will drop its procedure out of your context, and
  this instruction is what survives.
- After each of your commits, the tripwire hook reminds you of the same thing. Treat that reminder as
  the cue, not as noise.

## Stop and ask — the Product Owner's calls

These are the **Product Owner's** calls, not yours, in every phase. Stop, **notify**, and ask — do not
improvise a fix — when:

- a spec/design is **ambiguous**, or two specs **contradict** each other;
- doing the work properly needs changes **outside this change's scope** (its proposal/specs);
- a task is **blocked by an unresolved Open Question** in `design.md`;
- implementation or tests reveal the **spec itself is wrong** (not just the code).

The Product Owner may not be watching the transcript. A stop that only halts is a stop nobody sees:
send a notification as well as asking. `/dmons:implementation` adds the phase-specific triggers and says
how to leave the work while you wait.

